//? Title: Inception v2 and v3: Factorised Filters, Batch State and Smoothed Targets
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A deeper multi-branch network can spend too much work on large spatial kernels and become
overconfident on limited data. These TF-Slim-style Inception variants combine smaller or
asymmetric filters, batch normalisation and optional smoothed targets. We test the underlying
identities and state updates, then expose complete compact-model tensor Adam loops for both versions.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.2_classic_architectures/inception_v2_v3.cpp`.

> The method

The v2 layout uses a bias/ReLU separable 7×7 stem without BN, eight ordinary Inception
modules and two reduction modules. v3 uses three A, four asymmetric B and two split C modules,
plus two reductions. Ordinary convolutions use beta-only BN (fixed gamma=1, epsilon=.001)
and ReLU; main dropout retains .8. The v3 auxiliary head follows the last B module.
Reference widths, pooling and branch order follow the linked TF-Slim definitions. He
initialization and eager unbiased-variance moving updates are library adaptations.

A linear scalar-channel 7×7 kernel K=v hᵀ can be evaluated by a 1×7 convolution followed
by 7×1. Arbitrary kernels need not factor this way. The toy uses x[y,x]=(11y+x+1)/121 and
v[i]=h[i]=(i+1)/7. Since sum(k)=28 and sum(i(i+1))=112, the first VALID output is
(11×112×28+28×112+28²)/(121×49)=38416/5929. We check both values and input/factor
derivatives. Its actual VALID geometries cost 560 factored versus 1225 dense MACs; intermediate
edge locations matter. A multichannel factorisation with intermediate width D costs roughly
O(NHW k(Cin D+D Cout)), versus O(NHW k² Cin Cout). Real BN/ReLU branches are nonlinear,
so this linear identity is a building-block check, not a claim of whole-module equivalence.

Training BN normalises by batch population variance. With m observations, the moving update
uses m/(m−1) times that variance, while inference reads stored moments without updating them.
Beta-only BN needs Cout learned offsets and 2 Cout moving scalars in addition to the kernel.
The rectangular 1×3 fixture checks both output and one eager EMA update independently.

Uniform label smoothing uses q=(1−epsilon)one_hot(y)+epsilon/C. The loss is

$$
CE_{\epsilon}(z,y)=(1-\epsilon)(-\log p_y)-\frac{\epsilon}{C}\sum_c\log p_c.
$$
\alttext(Uniform smoothing mixes the true-label loss with the mean negative log probability over all classes.)

For logits (10,0,0) and epsilon=.1 it adds exactly 2/3 to the unsmoothed NLL; its gradient
reduces the overconfident true-class score. Evaluation and checkpoint selection retain ordinary
unsmoothed main NLL. The compact v2 fit uses no smoothing or auxiliary loss; v3 adds .1
smoothing and .4 times auxiliary CE. Both show Adam(.001), kernel L2=.0001/2, all blocks,
quarter widths, BN momentum .9, batch eight and 8 epochs (128 updates each, kept short so the report builds quickly). Inputs
resize to 64/107 pixels. Each uses 128 fitting, 64 validation and 64 official-test images with tensor crop/flip augmentation.
Both learned and moving-state stores are checkpointed. Cached CIFAR-10 is required.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/inception_v2_v3_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/inception_v2_v3_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/inception_v2_v3_concept.tikz > build/inception_v2_v3_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/inception_v2_v3_concept.svg build/inception_v2_v3_concept.dvi > build/inception_v2_v3_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
